+# Customer credit reviews
+
+Nothing could be run here (no PHP or database), so none of this has been executed. It was written against the code and the project map only.
+
+## What changed
+
+**New table `creditreviews`**
+- `install/sql/tables/creditreviews.sql` is the fresh-install definition. `sql/updates/44.php` creates the same table for upgrades, using `CreateTable('creditreviews', ...)`. The name matches the table, unlike `38.php`.
+- Each review holds the customer (`debtorno`), review date, reviewer's user id, outcome (0 kept, 1 raised, 2 lowered, 3 on hold), the limit before the review, the new limit, the hold status applied, the next review date, and the comments (`text`).
+- A foreign key `creditreviews_ibfk_1` ties `debtorno` to `debtorsmaster`, so a review cannot point at a customer that does not exist.
+
+**Upgrade and install registration**
+- `44.php` also registers the two scripts at security token 3 and adds the menu item "Customers Due For Credit Review" under AR > Reports (sequence 11). It calls `UpdateDBNo` only when `Errors == 0`, as the project rule says.
+- The same scripts and menu rows (roles 1 to 9) are added to `install/sql/data/scripts.sql`, `install/sql/data/menuitems.sql` and `install/sql/demo.sql`. A Demo=Yes install loads only `demo.sql`, so it needed them too.
+- A fresh install records `DBUpdateNumber` as the highest update file, which is now 44, so `44.php` never runs on a new install. That is why the install files carry the table and the rows.
+
+**New pages**
+- `CreditReviews.php?DebtorNo=` records a review, lists the customer's reviews newest first, and corrects or deletes one.
+- `CreditReviewsDue.php` lists customers whose next review date has passed.
+
+**Changes to existing files**
+- `SelectCustomer.php`: a "Credit reviews for this customer" link in the Customer Maintenance box. It is shown only to users whose tokens include the one that guards `CreditReviews.php`.
+- `Z_ChangeCustomerCode.php`: moves `creditreviews.debtorno` to the new code. It runs after the new `debtorsmaster` row is inserted and before the old row is deleted, so the foreign key holds.
+- `Customers.php`: deleting a customer now deletes its reviews first.
+- `doc/Manual/ManualAccountsReceivable.html`: new section "Customer Credit Reviews" (bookmark `CreditReviews`).
+
+## Behaviour
+
+- **Account changes.** A review that raises or lowers the limit sets `debtorsmaster.creditlimit`. A review that puts the account on hold sets `debtorsmaster.holdreason`. The review insert and the account update run in one transaction. The customer row is locked with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, so the recorded previous limit is the real one.
+- **Validation.**
+  - The review date must be valid and not in the future.
+  - The next review date is required and must be after the review date.
+  - The comments are required.
+  - "Raised" needs a new limit above the current one, and "lowered" needs one below it.
+  - "On hold" needs a credit status that disallows invoicing.
+- **Permissions.** Both pages are registered at token 3, "Credit notes and AR management". That is the token on `Customers.php`, which already lets a user change the credit limit and hold status. Page security is enforced by `session.php`, so no check was added inside the pages.
+
+## Ambiguities and how I resolved them
+
+1. **Who may do it.** The request says "the people who look after customer credit". The seed data has no credit-controller token or role. I reused token 3 rather than invent a token that no role holds. The roles that hold it in the seed are Purchasing Officer, AR Clerk, Accountant and System Administrator. An administrator can narrow this by giving a credit-control role token 3 and taking it from the others. It could also be changed to a new token if one is wanted.
+2. **"Who did it".** This is the logged-in user, taken automatically and shown with their real name. It is not a free-text field. A review entered on behalf of someone else would show the person who typed it.
+3. **Correcting a review.** I read "correct a review entered by mistake" as edit or delete the record.
+   - Editing changes the dates and comments only. The outcome, limits and hold are fixed once saved.
+   - Neither editing nor deleting reverses the effect on the customer account. The page and the manual say so.
+   - The alternative, restoring the previous limit when a review is deleted, goes wrong once later reviews or manual edits have changed the account. To fix the account, record a new review.
+4. **Taking an account off hold.** The four outcomes do not include it, so a review never releases a hold. That is done on `Customers.php`.
+5. **Which hold status.** The request says only "on hold". The user picks the status, limited to those with `dissallowinvoices = 1` in Credit Status. If none exist, hold cannot be recorded until one is set up.
+6. **Overdue list.**
+   - "Next review date has passed" means before today.
+   - Only each customer's latest review counts, by review date and then id, because newer reviews supersede older dates.
+   - A customer with no review at all is not listed. There is no date to pass, and listing every unreviewed customer would swamp the report.
+7. **Next review date is required** on every review, including "on hold". Otherwise a customer could drop out of the overdue list, which is the "reviews are forgotten" problem in the request.
+8. **"Everything that can happen to the customer's account".** I found two operations that remove or rekey a customer: the code-change utility and customer delete, now both handled. Branch changes (`Z_ChangeBranchCode.php`) do not matter, because reviews are keyed on the customer only. Deleting a customer is already refused when it has transactions, orders or sales analysis. The delete page still goes ahead for a customer that only has contracts or web users. That is existing behaviour and I left it. The reviews are deleted in either case, so none are orphaned.
+9. **Amounts** are shown with 0 decimals, as `Customers.php` does for credit limits.
+
+## Not done or worth knowing
+
+- `AuditScripts.php` is absent from the seed `scripts.sql`. That is an existing gap, and I did not touch it.
+- Double-submitting the new-review form records two reviews. This matches the other maintenance pages and I added no guard.
+- The locale `.po` files were not updated for the new strings.
