 <p>Customer notes containing any text - a web-link if required and a date can be entered from the Select Customer screen - for the selected customer. The notes are displayed in sequence of priority - so pressing issues appear at the top of the list. These notes might contain information about recent contact with the customer or disputes. There is no checking on the content of the notes and no limit to either the number of notes nor the number of characters that can be used.</p>
 
+<h2><a id="CreditReviews">Customer Credit Reviews</a></h2>
+
+<p>A credit review is the record of the credit controller looking at how a customer pays and deciding what happens to the customer's credit. Reviews are recorded from the Select Customer screen, using the link "Credit reviews for this customer" in the Customer Maintenance box. The link, and the review page itself, are available only to users whose role holds security token 3 (Credit notes and AR management), the same token that allows the credit limit of a customer to be changed on the Customers page. Users without that token do not see the reviews.</p>
+
+<p>Each review records:</p>
+
+<ul>
+    <li>the review date, which cannot be in the future, and the user who entered the review - this is taken from the user logged in and cannot be typed in;</li>
+
+    <li>the outcome - <em>Limit kept</em>, <em>Limit raised</em>, <em>Limit lowered</em> or <em>Account on hold</em>;</li>
+
+    <li>the new credit limit, for the outcomes <em>Limit raised</em> and <em>Limit lowered</em>. A raised limit must be more, and a lowered limit less, than the limit the customer has when the review is saved. The limit before the review is recorded with the review;</li>
+
+    <li>the credit status to apply, for the outcome <em>Account on hold</em>. Only credit statuses that disallow invoicing are offered - they are defined in Credit Status in the Setup menu, so at least one such status must exist;</li>
+
+    <li>the date of the next review, which must be after the review date, and the comments of the controller. Both are required. Comments can run to several paragraphs.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>When the outcome is <em>Limit raised</em> or <em>Limit lowered</em> the credit limit of the customer account is changed to the new limit, and when it is <em>Account on hold</em> the credit status of the customer account is changed to the chosen status. The review and the change to the account are saved together or not at all. <em>Limit kept</em> changes nothing on the account. A review never takes an account off hold - do that on the Customers page, and record a review saying why.</p>
+
+<p>The past reviews of the customer are listed on the same page, newest first. A review entered by mistake can be corrected, which changes its dates and comments only, or deleted. Neither correcting nor deleting a review undoes what the review did to the customer account - the outcome, the limit and the hold are left as they are. To put the account right, record a new review, or change the customer on the Customers page.</p>
+
+<p>The report "Customers Due For Credit Review" in the Accounts Receivable reports menu lists the customers whose next review date has passed, most overdue first, with a link to record the review. For each customer only the latest review counts, so a customer drops off the list as soon as a new review with a future next review date is recorded. A customer who has never been reviewed does not appear, as there is no review date to pass - record a first review for them.</p>
+
+<p>Credit reviews belong to the customer. If the customer code is changed with the utility Change A Customer Code, the reviews move to the new code, and if a customer is deleted its reviews are deleted with it.</p>
+
 <h2><a id="CustomerTypeNotes">Customer Type Notes</a></h2>
 
 <p>As well as unlimited notes on individual customers, webERP has the functionality to store an unlimited number of notes on groups of customers based on the debtor type field. As with the notes on individual customers , these notes are displayed in order of priority, and can contain a URL as well as the free form note.</p>
